I'll add drag support to the tab so it registers the chat id as `sprintTaskId` in the data transfer when dragged.

## Direct Recommendation
Make the tab `draggable` only when it carries a chat, and in the `dragstart` handler write the chat id into `event.dataTransfer` under the key `sprintTaskId`. This keeps non-chat tabs (plain apps/tools) non-draggable and avoids polluting the drag payload.

## Changes

- **Template:** Add `:draggable="!!chat"` and `@dragstart="onDragStart"` on the root element. Add a subtle cursor hint (`cursor-grab`) so users know the tab is draggable.
- **Methods:** Add `onDragStart(event)` that guards on `chat` and writes `sprintTaskId`.

```vue src/components/desktop/Tab.vue
<script setup>
import AppIcon from '../apps/AppIcon.vue'
</script>
<template>
  <div
    class="codx-tab flex gap-1 items-center justify-between px-1 py-0.5 border rounded-xl w-40 text-md"
    :draggable="!!chat"
    :class="{ 'cursor-grab active:cursor-grabbing': !!chat }"
    @dragstart="onDragStart"
  >
    <div class="w-4/5 flex gap-1 items-center truncate overflow-hidden">
      <!-- Pass loading state to AppIcon so it shows the ring inside the icon area -->
      <AppIcon :app="app" :loading="isUpdating" />
      <div class="grow truncate overflow-hidden">{{ tabName }}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="click hover:text-warning shrink-0 mr-2" @click="onClose">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-xmark fa-sm"></i>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>
<script>

export default {
  props: ['params'],
  data() {
    return {
      project: null,
    }
  },
  async created() {
    this.project = this.$projects.allProjectsById[this.projectId]
  },
  computed: {
    chat() {
      const chatId = this.params?.params?.chat?.id
      return this.$storex.chats.chats[chatId]
    },
    app() {
      return this.params.params.app
    },
    tabName() {
      return this.chat?.name || 
        this.app?.title ||
        this.params.params.tabName
    },
    api() {
      return this.params.api
    },
    containerApi() {
      return this.params.containerApi
    },
    panel() {
      return this.api.panel
    },
    projectId() {
      return this.params.params.project_id
    },
    isUpdating() {
      return this.$storex.chats.isChatUpdating(this.chat?.id)
    }
  },
  methods: {
    onDragStart(event) {
      // Only register a sprint task id when the tab is backed by a chat
      if (!this.chat) return
      event.dataTransfer.setData('sprintTaskId', this.chat.id)
      event.dataTransfer.effectAllowed = 'move'
    },
    onClose() {
      this.containerApi.removePanel(this.panel)
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

## Specifications

| Aspect | Value |
|--------|-------|
| Draggable condition | `chat` exists (chat-backed tabs only) |
| Data key | `sprintTaskId` |
| Data value | `this.chat.id` |
| Drag effect | `move` |
| Cursor (idle / dragging) | `cursor-grab` / `active:cursor-grabbing` |
| Transition | none (native drag handles feedback) |

## Anti-Patterns

- ❌ Always setting `draggable="true"` — plain app tabs shouldn't be draggable and would emit confusing drop payloads.
- ❌ Writing the id in `dragend` instead of `dragstart` — `dataTransfer` is only writable during `dragstart` (and is cleared synchronously after the drag ends).
- ❌ Using browser-specific types like `text/plain` for your own id — a custom key (`sprintTaskId`) avoids collision with other drag sources on the page.

## Accessibility Checklist

- [x] Drag is an **enhancement** — the tab remains fully operable via click/keyboard (`onClose` unaffected).
- [x] No focus ring regression — added classes only set `cursor`.
- [ ] Consider adding a keyboard alternative (e.g. a context menu "Add to sprint") for users who can't drag.

## Implementation Steps

1. Add `:draggable="!!chat"` to the root `<div>`.
2. Add the `cursor-grab active:cursor-grabbing` binding (via `:class`) so the affordance shows only for chat tabs.
3. Wire `@dragstart="onDragStart"` on the root element.
4. Add the `onDragStart` method that writes `event.dataTransfer.setData('sprintTaskId', this.chat.id)`.
5. On the drop target side, read it back with `event.dataTransfer.getData('sprintTaskId')`.
